<td id="S7.T2.5.17.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;">13.53</td>
<td id="S7.T2.5.17.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;">62.10</td>
<td id="S7.T2.5.17.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;">9.79</td></tr>
<tr id="S7.T2.5.18" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S7.T2.5.18.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="S7.T2.5.18.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">smolagents</span></td>
<td id="S7.T2.5.18.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;">68.64</td>
<td id="S7.T2.5.18.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;">38.01</td>
<td id="S7.T2.5.18.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;">4.34</td>
<td id="S7.T2.5.18.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;">2.16</td></tr>
<tr id="S7.T2.5.19" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S7.T2.5.19.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="S7.T2.5.19.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Pneuma-Seeker</span></td>
<td id="S7.T2.5.19.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;">94.41</td>
<td id="S7.T2.5.19.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;">40.27</td>
<td id="S7.T2.5.19.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;">3.36</td>
<td id="S7.T2.5.19.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;">1.31</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.3. </span>RQ3: Microbenchmark</h3>

<div id="S7.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We complement RQ1 and RQ2 with experiments designed to stress-test design trade-offs. These experiments analyze (i) the answer quality on a much weaker, non-reasoning LLM, (ii) memory behavior and runtime–memory trade-offs induced by <math id="S7.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{T},S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{T},S)</annotation></semantics></math>, and (iii) the retrieval recall of <span id="S7.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Pneuma-Seeker</span>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S7.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">7.3.1. </span>Answer Quality with a Non-Reasoning LLM</h4>

<div id="S7.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We replace the LLM with a much weaker, non-reasoning LLM, <code id="S7.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_verbatim ltx_font_typewriter">gpt-4.1-mini-20-</code> <code id="S7.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_verbatim ltx_font_typewriter">25-04-14</code>, and re-evaluate answer quality. We re-run all systems on all benchmarks besides Astronomy, since <span id="S7.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DS-Guru</span> cannot run on it, then report the average answer quality across all datasets in Figure <a href="#S7.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 7.3.1. Answer Quality with a Non-Reasoning LLM ‣ 7.3. RQ3: Microbenchmark ‣ 7. Quantitative Evaluation ‣ Pneuma-Seeker: Relational Reification of Information Needs for Agentic Data Discovery and Preparation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. All systems experience degradation in answer quality with the non-reasoning LLM, but <span id="S7.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Pneuma-Seeker</span> still achieves the highest answer quality: <math id="S7.SS3.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="49.95\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>49.95</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">49.95\%</annotation></semantics></math>, which is <math id="S7.SS3.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.14" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1.14</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.14</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS3.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12.61" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>12.61</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12.61</annotation></semantics></math> percentage points higher than <span id="S7.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">smolagents</span> and <span id="S7.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DS-Guru</span>, respectively.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S7.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.10747v1/rq1_microbenchmark_models.svg" id="S7.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/236;" width="476" height="236"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S7.F5.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 5</span>. </span><span id="S7.F5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Answer Quality with a Non-Reasoning LLM</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">7.3.2. </span>Memory Usage and Runtime–Memory Trade-offs</h4>

<div id="S7.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We study the feasibility of running the systems locally with limited memory (16 GB, in our case). Across datasets, <span id="S7.SS3.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Pneuma-Seeker</span> exhibits relatively stable memory usage and is often comparable to or lower than the baselines.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS3.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS3.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The largest discrepancy appears on the Wildfire dataset: <span id="S7.SS3.SSS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DS-Guru</span> loads all rows of all tables into memory and peaks at 5.3 GB, whereas <span id="S7.SS3.SSS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">smolagents</span> and <span id="S7.SS3.SSS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Pneuma-Seeker</span> use only 186 MB and 149 MB, respectively. Similar trends hold on other datasets: for example, on Biomedical, <span id="S7.SS3.SSS2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DS-Guru</span> peaks at 567 MB, while <span id="S7.SS3.SSS2.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">smolagents</span> and <span id="S7.SS3.SSS2.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Pneuma-Seeker</span> use 135 MB and 186 MB; on Legal, <span id="S7.SS3.SSS2.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Pneuma-Seeker</span> peaks at 116 MB, compared to 43 MB for <span id="S7.SS3.SSS2.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">smolagents</span> and 25 MB for <span id="S7.SS3.SSS2.p2.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DS-Guru</span>. Overall, the relative memory differences across the remaining datasets follow similar patterns.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS3.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS3.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS3.SSS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Scalability.</span>  We also compare memory usage on the scalability dataset (Section <a href="#S7.SS2.SSS2" title="7.2.2. Scalability ‣ 7.2. RQ2: Cost and Scalability ‣ 7. Quantitative Evaluation ‣ Pneuma-Seeker: Relational Reification of Information Needs for Agentic Data Discovery and Preparation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.2.2</span></a>). At larger scales, <span id="S7.SS3.SSS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Pneuma-Seeker</span> consistently consumes the least memory. For example, at the 1 GB scale, <span id="S7.SS3.SSS2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Pneuma-Seeker</span> uses 135 MB, whereas <span id="S7.SS3.SSS2.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DS-Guru</span> and <span id="S7.SS3.SSS2.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">smolagents</span> use 4.4 GB and 916 MB, respectively (Figure <a href="#S7.F6.sf1" title="In Figure 6 ‣ 7.3.2. Memory Usage and Runtime–Memory Trade-offs ‣ 7.3. RQ3: Microbenchmark ‣ 7. Quantitative Evaluation ‣ Pneuma-Seeker: Relational Reification of Information Needs for Agentic Data Discovery and Preparation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6(a)</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS3.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS3.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We observe irregularities in the runtime and memory usage of <span id="S7.SS3.SSS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">smolagents</span> when scaling from 1 GB to 1.9 GB. The observed reduction in runtime and memory usage at 1.9 GB is due to an internal optimization triggered by data-integrity errors. Specifically, the smaller dataset was processed using a standard, resource-intensive loading strategy, whereas the larger dataset contained noisy strings that caused the agent to switch to a more selective pipeline. This alternative pipeline performs column pruning and hash-based filtering, resulting in lower memory usage and computational overhead than the generic processing strategy applied to the smaller dataset.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S7.F6" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S7.F6.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.10747v1/rq2_scalability_peak_delta_rss.svg" id="S7.F6.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/237;" width="476" height="237"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S7.F6.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S7.F6.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Scalability of Memory Usage</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S7.F6.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.10747v1/pareto_runtime_vs_memory.svg" id="S7.F6.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/272;" width="476" height="272"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S7.F6.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S7.F6.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Memory Usage vs. Runtime</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S7.F6.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.10747v1/rq1_retrieval_recall.svg" id="S7.F6.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/167;" width="476" height="167"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S7.F6.sf3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(c)</span> </span><span id="S7.F6.sf3.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Retrieval Recall of <span id="S7.F6.sf3.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Pneuma-Retriever</span> </span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S7.F6.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 6</span>. </span><span id="S7.F6.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Scalability, memory–runtime trade-offs, and retrieval recall.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S7.SS3.SSS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS3.SSS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS3.SSS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Runtime vs. Memory Usage.</span> 
Overall, as shown in Figure <a href="#S7.F6.sf2" title="In Figure 6 ‣ 7.3.2. Memory Usage and Runtime–Memory Trade-offs ‣ 7.3. RQ3: Microbenchmark ‣ 7. Quantitative Evaluation ‣ Pneuma-Seeker: Relational Reification of Information Needs for Agentic Data Discovery and Preparation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6(b)</span></a>, <span id="S7.SS3.SSS2.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Pneuma-Seeker</span> tends to operate in a low-memory but higher-runtime regime. <span id="S7.SS3.SSS2.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">smolagents</span> is also low-memory and, on average, exhibits lower runtime than <span id="S7.SS3.SSS2.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Pneuma-Seeker</span>. <span id="S7.SS3.SSS2.p5.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DS-Guru</span> achieves low runtime and memory usage only when the dataset is small.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS3.SSS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS3.SSS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">This reflects a fundamental trade-off: introducing <math id="S7.SS3.SSS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{T},S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{T},S)</annotation></semantics></math> requires <span id="S7.SS3.SSS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Pneuma-Seeker</span> to perform additional structured processing, whereas the baselines attempt to directly synthesize answers. The additional overhead introduced by <math id="S7.SS3.SSS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{T},S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{T},S)</annotation></semantics></math> manifests primarily as increased runtime rather than increased memory footprint. This cost is compensated by the improvements in answer accuracy and explainability enabled by <math id="S7.SS3.SSS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{T},S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{T},S)</annotation></semantics></math>, aligning with <span id="S7.SS3.SSS2.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">R<a href="#S2.I1.i1" title="1st item ‣ 2.3. Solution Requirements ‣ 2. Information Needs on Tabular Data ‣ Pneuma-Seeker: Relational Reification of Information Needs for Agentic Data Discovery and Preparation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">•</span> ‣ <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a></span> and <span id="S7.SS3.SSS2.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">R<a href="#S2.I1.i2" title="2nd item ‣ 2.3. Solution Requirements ‣ 2. Information Needs on Tabular Data ‣ Pneuma-Seeker: Relational Reification of Information Needs for Agentic Data Discovery and Preparation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">•</span> ‣ <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a></span>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS3.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">7.3.3. </span>Retrieval Recall as an Upper Bound on Answer Quality</h4>

<div id="S7.SS3.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS3.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The baselines have access to all tables in each dataset, whereas <span id="S7.SS3.SSS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Pneuma-Seeker</span> relies on tables retrieved by <span id="S7.SS3.SSS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Pneuma-Retriever</span>. Consequently, the answer quality of <span id="S7.SS3.SSS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Pneuma-Seeker</span> is upper-bounded by the recall of <span id="S7.SS3.SSS3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Pneuma-Retriever</span>: if a required table is not retrieved, <span id="S7.SS3.SSS3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Pneuma-Seeker</span> cannot utilize it downstream. To isolate and quantify the impact of retrieval on end-to-end answer quality, we explicitly evaluate the recall of <span id="S7.SS3.SSS3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Pneuma-Retriever</span> and compare it against <span id="S7.SS3.SSS3.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Pneuma</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Balaka et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and <span id="S7.SS3.SSS3.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Pneuma</span> + content search (Section <a href="#S5.SS3" title="5.3. Retriever ‣ 5. The Pneuma-Seeker System ‣ Pneuma-Seeker: Relational Reification of Information Needs for Agentic Data Discovery and Preparation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>). We note that <span id="S7.SS3.SSS3.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Pneuma-Retriever</span> corresponds to <span id="S7.SS3.SSS3.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Pneuma</span> augmented with content search and table enumeration.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS3.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS3.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Figure <a href="#S7.F6.sf3" title="In Figure 6 ‣ 7.3.2. Memory Usage and Runtime–Memory Trade-offs ‣ 7.3. RQ3: Microbenchmark ‣ 7. Quantitative Evaluation ‣ Pneuma-Seeker: Relational Reification of Information Needs for Agentic Data Discovery and Preparation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6(c)</span></a>, <span id="S7.SS3.SSS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Pneuma</span> + content search slightly improves recall over <span id="S7.SS3.SSS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Pneuma</span> on the Legal and Wildfire datasets, by <math id="S7.SS3.SSS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\%</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS3.SSS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\%</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. In contrast, <span id="S7.SS3.SSS3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Pneuma-Retriever</span> achieves <math id="S7.SS3.SSS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>100</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100\%</annotation></semantics></math> recall on all datasets except Legal (<math id="S7.SS3.SSS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="94\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>94</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">94\%</annotation></semantics></math>). The recall gap between <span id="S7.SS3.SSS3.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Pneuma-Retriever</span> and the other retrievers is particularly pronounced on the Astronomy, Environment, and Legal datasets.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS3.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS3.SSS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The primary source of this discrepancy is that <span id="S7.SS3.SSS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Pneuma-Retriever</span> allows <span id="S7.SS3.SSS3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Conductor</span> and <span id="S7.SS3.SSS3.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Materializer</span> to further enumerate tables with similar names using regex-based matching, which is not captured by standard top-<math id="S7.SS3.SSS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> retrieval. For example, the query <span id="S7.SS3.SSS3.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“Does there exist a metropolitan area in which the number of reports of identity theft exceeded the number of reports of fraud in 2024?”</span> requires retrieving 104 tables corresponding to fraud and identity-theft reports across all U.S. states. Top-<math id="S7.SS3.SSS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> retrievers systematically under-retrieve in such settings.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">8. </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first introduced an early version of <span id="S8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Pneuma-Seeker</span> as part of the Pneuma project <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Balaka and Fernandez, 2026</a>)</cite>. The system has since evolved along three axes: (i) using Python script (<math id="S8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>) rather than SQL queries (<math id="S8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>) to support more expressive computation over <math id="S8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math>; (ii) introducing <span id="S8.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DBService</span> to enable database-backed actions to improve scalability; and (iii) adding Context Extraction as an action for micro-context management, altering how <span id="S8.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Conductor</span> and <span id="S8.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Materializer</span> interact with retrieved tables by surfacing doubts and questioning assumptions when defining <math id="S8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{T},S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{T},S)</annotation></semantics></math> and materializing <math id="S8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math>. We next situate <span id="S8.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Pneuma-Seeker</span> within three related research areas.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sensemaking and Exploratory Search.</span> 
Sensemaking and exploratory search frame analysis as iterative processes of foraging, hypothesis formation, and synthesis with feedback and intermediate representations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pirolli and Card, 2005</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Marchionini, 2006</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hearst, 2009</a>)</cite>. This perspective motivates <span id="S8.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Pneuma-Seeker</span> ’s framing of tabular QA as interactive sensemaking rather than one-shot query answering. Recent mixed-initiative systems such as <span id="S8.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DocWrangler</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shankar et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, <span id="S8.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ScholarMate</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ye et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, and <span id="S8.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Sensecape</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Suh et al., 2023</a>)</cite> similarly support iterative refinement, inspection, and evolving information needs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Agentic Systems for Tabular Question Answering (QA).</span> 
Recent LLM-based systems move beyond single-step retrieval or end-to-end generation (e.g., RAG <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lewis et al., 2020</a>)</cite>) toward agentic architectures that integrate planning, tool use, and iterative reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yue, 2025</a>)</cite>. These systems implement reason–act–reflect loops (e.g., ReAct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al., 2023</a>)</cite>) and orchestrate tools such as database queries, code execution, and web search <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Saadaoui and Alonso, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang and Yuan, 2025</a>)</cite>. In Tabular QA, <span id="S8.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ReAcTable</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, <span id="S8.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Chain-of-Table</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, and <span id="S8.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TableZoomer</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xiong et al., 2025</a>)</cite> leverage intermediate programs and iterative validation, but assume small curated table sets or single-table reasoning. In contrast, <span id="S8.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Pneuma-Seeker</span> couples agentic reasoning with large-scale data discovery and multi-table reasoning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Tabular Data Discovery.</span> 
Tabular data discovery spans metadata-driven search, view identification, schema matching, and natural-language interfaces. Some systems emphasize structured discovery and joinability analysis, such as <span id="S8.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Aurum</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Castro Fernandez et al., 2018a</a>)</cite>, <span id="S8.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Auctus</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Castelo et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, <span id="S8.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Ver</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gong et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, and FREYJA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Maynou et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. Others incorporate learned representations for natural-language and multi-table discovery, such as <span id="S8.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Solo</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang and Castro Fernandez, 2023</a>)</cite> and <span id="S8.p4.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Octopus</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li and Galhotra, 2026</a>)</cite>. Our prior system, <span id="S8.p4.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Pneuma</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Balaka et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, underlies the discovery component in <span id="S8.p4.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Pneuma-Seeker</span>; here we extend it to prioritize recall beyond top-<math id="S8.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> to support multi-table reasoning, as downstream agentic performance is bounded by discovery completeness.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S9" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">9. </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This paper presented relational reification and a system, <span id="S9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Pneuma-Seeker</span>, that implements this idea within an LLM-powered agentic system to help users articulate their information needs and answer them. Our results show that representing evolving intent as an explicit <math id="S9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{T},S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{T},S)</annotation></semantics></math> pair improves answer quality while enabling inspectability. Taken together, these findings suggest that relational reification is a practical foundation for trustworthy LLM-mediated data discovery and preparation.</p>
</div>
</section>
